12056e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.25509878993034363,-8.666139910928905e-05,-0.0009964506607502699,-0.0036601710598915815,-0.11400040239095688,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-2.4914430468925275e-05,-0.49005353450775146,0.0,-0.003590923734009266,-0.12780849635601044,0.0,0.0,-8.022463589441031e-05,-1.0728830375228426e-06,0.0,0.0,-1.311301275563892e-06,-2.9802276912960224e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,279,19703,315,198,78045,89,61,18,284,220,17,488,220,17,72,59,60,1371,400,64,62,16,488,18668,62,16,4779,400,64,62,17,488,18668,62,17,4779,323,400,64,62,18,488,18668,62,18,2418,220,22603,400,64,62,16,264,62,17,264,62,18,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet the roots of\n\\[z^3 = 2 + 2i\\]be $a_1 + ib_1,$ $a_2 + ib_2,$ and $a_3 + ib_3.$  Compute $a_1 a_2 a_3.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ff005de370cff678c70e2b6ba7f332ae5f3edfb147f2e3fb77cf55d8c9c46af5"},"seconds":61.403159856796265,"seed":817146963690983089,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we first need to express the complex number \\(2 + 2i\\) in polar form. The polar form of a complex number \\(z = x + yi\\) is given by \\(z = r(\\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta)\\), where \\(r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\) and \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(y/x)\\).\n\nFor \\(2 + 2i\\):\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 4} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\tan^{-1}(2/2) = \\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\]\n\nThus, we can write \\(2 + 2i\\) as:\n\\[ 2 + 2i = 2\\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} + i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\]\n\nThe equation \\(z^3 = 2 + 2i\\) can now be rewritten as:\n\\[ z^3 = 2\\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} + i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\]\n\nThe general solution for the cube roots of a complex number \\(re^{i\\theta}\\) is given by:\n\\[ z_k = \\sqrt[3]{r} \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\theta + 2k\\pi}{3} \\right) + i \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\theta + 2k\\pi}{3} \\right) \\right) \\]\nfor \\(k = 0, 1, 2\\).\n\nFor our specific case, \\(r = 2\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), so:\n\\[ z_k = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2k\\pi}{3} \\right) + i \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2k\\pi}{3} \\right) \\right) \\]\n\\[ z_k = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3} \\right) + i \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3} \\right) \\right) \\]\n\nThe three roots are:\n\\[ z_0 = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} + i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} \\right) \\]\n\\[ z_1 = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos \\frac{3\\pi}{4} + i \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\]\n\\[ z_2 = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos \\frac{17\\pi}{12} + i \\sin \\frac{17\\pi}{12} \\right) \\]\n\nThe product of the roots of the polynomial \\(z^3 - (2+2i) = 0\\) is given by \\((-1)^3 \\cdot \\frac{-2-2i}{1} = 2 + 2i\\). However, since the polynomial is monic (leading coefficient is 1), the product of the roots is simply the constant term with the sign changed, which is \\(-(-2-2i) = 2 + 2i\\).\n\nSince the roots are \\(z_0, z_1, z_2\\), and the product of the roots of the polynomial \\(z^3 - (2+2i) = 0\\) is \\(2 + 2i\\), we have:\n\\[ a_1 a_2 a_3 = \\text{Re}(z_0) \\text{Re}(z_1) \\text{Re}(z_2) \\]\nBut since the product of the roots in their complex form gives us the real part product as the real part of \\(2 + 2i\\), we have:\n\\[ a_1 a_2 a_3 = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
